WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:07.700
In 2010, then-governor of California Arnold
Schwarzenegger visited Japan, Korea, and China,

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.510
marveling at their high-speed trains.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:15.000
China’s, though still in their infancy,
were already faster than any in America — by

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.100
about 120 miles, or 190 kilometers, an hour.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:25.710
California’s ambitions were modest by comparison
— to build one line, from San Francisco

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:32.759
to LA — about 70% the length of China’s
longest, by 2020, 10 years later.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:40.630
A second phase would connect LA to San Diego
down South and Merced to Sacramento up North.

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:45.600
Ten years later, California was somehow still
10 years away.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:52.060
Meanwhile, the cost had grown from $33 billion
in the 2008 proposal to $53 billion in 2012,

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:58.620
to 77 in 2018 — two and a third times the
original estimate.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.530
Still, it gets worse…

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:06.359
In an embarrassing illustration of American
bureaucracy, its trains, if and when they

00:01:06.359 --> 00:01:11.750
ever exist, will have two sets of doors at
different heights for different stations,

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.340
after failing to compromise with Caltrain.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.529
Then, there’s China.

00:01:17.529 --> 00:01:22.880
While California was busy bickering over the
height of its doors and fending off lawsuits,

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.799
China put its head down and just built.

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:32.310
Even after becoming #1 in high-speed rail,
it never looked back, charging forward until

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:35.969
it had surpassed every other nation put together.

00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:40.009
Then two-thirds of the entire world total.

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:44.310
More impressive is that it did all this as
a middle-income country.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:51.579
At the time it began construction, its per
capita GDP was just $3,400 current US dollars.

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:56.749
If you adjust the length of each nation’s
high-speed rail by GDP per capita, few others

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:59.119
are even visible.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:05.360
But trains are only one, high-profile symbol
of a much larger pattern — that, while China’s

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.750
methods may be crude and controversial, they
are, ultimately, more efficient.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:15.439
The country has dramatically more electric
buses, for instance, than anywhere else on

00:02:15.439 --> 00:02:18.610
the planet, by a crazy margin.

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:24.610
One city even built an elevated bus lane,
allowing them to zoom past traffic jams.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:31.140
So too does its number of electric charging
stations dwarf all other regions by comparison.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:36.250
While Congress hems and haws about “big
tech” monopolies, Chinese authorities just

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:39.870
snap their fingers and break them up.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:45.700
American politicians, it’s often said, think
only one election cycle ahead, whereas Chinese

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.890
leaders are free to plan 50 or 100-year “ grand
strategies”.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:55.040
…Or, so it may seem.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.329
