00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.849
There are three ingredients to successful
high-speed rail:

00:03:06.849 --> 00:03:11.450
First, and most obvious, you need lots of
people.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:16.829
Not just a large number, but, ideally, a string
of high-density clusters.

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:17.989
Check.

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:23.609
The vast majority of the 100 Chinese cities
with a population over one million are, quite

00:03:23.609 --> 00:03:27.249
conveniently, clumped together on its Eastern,
coastal side.

00:03:27.249 --> 00:03:31.450
Second, it can’t be too expensive.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:34.040
Again, no problem.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.560
In addition to the much lower cost of labor,
the sheer scale of China’s plans allowed

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:43.749
construction companies to optimize every step
of the process and amortize large investments

00:03:43.749 --> 00:03:47.200
over a significantly longer time and project
horizon.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.379
A report by the World Bank found that its
average cost was “at least 40% cheaper”

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:55.060
than the European equivalent.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:04:00.000
It didn’t hurt that everything, up to and
including the trains themselves, were built

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.530
in China.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:06.319
Third, and finally, a good candidate for high-speed
rail is rich.

00:04:06.319 --> 00:04:09.700
This, it most certainly was not.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:15.430
But with annual GDP growth over 10%, at the
time, it wasn’t crazy to think it would

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:17.359
soon get there.

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:22.910
Its first few routes connected wealthy 1st,
2nd, and 3rd-tier cities like Nanjing, the

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:26.420
5th richest, to Guangzhou, the 13th.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:29.890
They were also the right length.

00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:34.720
For trips shorter than roughly 100 miles,
it generally makes more sense to drive or

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:40.160
take a bus, especially given that many stations
are far outside city centers.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:47.250
Conversely, for trips longer than about 750
miles, it’s usually preferable to fly.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:52.360
These journeys take upwards of 5 hours on
even the fastest trains.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:05:00.470
The space between 100 and 750 miles, but especially
below 500, is the goldilocks zone for high-speed

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:06.090
rail — too far to drive, yet not far enough
to fly.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:10.550
8 out of its first 10 routes had total lengths
within these bounds.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:13.919
It all seemed pretty sensible.

00:05:13.919 --> 00:05:18.740
And had it stopped there, in 2010, it would
still, today, have the longest high-speed

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.310
rail network of any country.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:26.150
Even building nothing new for the next 12
years, it would still go down in history as

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:30.880
a tremendous accomplishment in speed, scale,
and cost.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:33.610
Instead, China kept building.

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:34.759
And building.

00:05:34.759 --> 00:05:35.889
And building.

00:05:35.889 --> 00:05:37.230
And building.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:40.690
Now, this wasn’t its original intention.

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:46.759
Far from a grand, strategic plan, it, along
with much of what we know as “modern China”

00:05:46.759 --> 00:05:52.639
today, were reactions — reactions to the
2008 financial crisis.

00:05:52.639 --> 00:06:00.910
45% of its nearly $600 billion stimulus package
went toward “transport and power infrastructure”.

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:05.580
It seemed like the perfect time to seize the
opportunity and get ahead, rather than sit

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:11.260
back and wait until its infrastructure was
already crumbling, like the U.S.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.629
But it was still, at its core, a gamble.

00:06:14.629 --> 00:06:19.849
One, giant assumption that demand would materialize
and that increased interconnectivity would

00:06:19.849 --> 00:06:22.629
drive greater economic growth.

00:06:22.629 --> 00:06:26.500
This was not as sound a bet as it may seem.

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:31.110
For anyone living in the U.S. or other rich
countries, high-speed rail probably looks

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:36.340
like a cheaper, more environmentally-sound
alternative to air travel.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:41.400
But the data clearly shows that, in China,
the biggest competitor to high-speed trains

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:45.150
are older, cheaper, low-speed trains.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:50.990
In a 2015 survey, about half of all passengers
said they otherwise would have taken conventional

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:52.090
trains.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:55.620
Only a small minority say they would’ve
flown.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:58.430
The problem is cost.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:04.810
One study found the average cost to build
a 350 km/hr line was 90% higher than even

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:11.480
a two hundred and fifty kilometer one — which
is still, by the way, considered “high-speed”.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:17.039
This is, in large part, because the faster
lines use elevated tracks, which, given China’s

00:07:17.039 --> 00:07:22.220
softer soils, would compress and sink over
time, causing a safety risk.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:28.349
To prevent this, the fastest trains require
extensive use of bridges and viaducts, which

00:07:28.349 --> 00:07:32.870
are significantly more expensive than roadbed,
made of sand, rock, and gravel.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:36.960
And that’s just the construction.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.240
These are not LEGOs — build once and play
forever.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:43.169
They also have operating costs.

00:07:43.169 --> 00:07:46.860
Energy use is not linear with respect to speed.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:52.599
Electricity consumption increases much faster
with every additional kilometer per hour.

00:07:52.599 --> 00:07:58.229
All of this is to say: that extra bit of speed
is really expensive.

00:07:58.229 --> 00:08:02.370
Conventional trains cost roughly 2 to 3 cents
per kilometer.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.219
High-speed, triple that.

00:08:05.219 --> 00:08:10.569
Take a random route on a random day — let’s
say Guangzhou to Shanghai.

00:08:10.569 --> 00:08:16.790
The fastest journey takes 6 hours, 48 minutes
and costs $124.

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:23.810
The slow journey takes nearly a full day but
costs just $31.

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:28.719
Anyone watching this video would likely jump
at the opportunity to spend just 93 more dollars

00:08:28.719 --> 00:08:32.470
to save a whole 16 hours of travel time.

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:35.200
But the same can’t be assumed in China.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:41.000
Though there’s no definitive “Value of
Time”, most estimates use the average hourly

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.310
wage for business travelers, and no more than
half that amount for leisure.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:51.750
A quick back-of-the-envelope calculation reveals
the 5.8 US dollars per hour saved would be

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:57.000
just marginally worthwhile for the average
business traveler, but not for the average

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.350
leisure traveler.

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:04.610
Another way of visualizing this is by splitting
the population into 10 equal-sized groups

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:06.650
by income.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:14.040
What this shows is that $93 is at least half
of weekly income for about 60% of China’s

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.140
population.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:19.810
Of course, in reality, a disproportionate
number of the remaining, highest-income travelers

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:23.500
will simply opt for air travel instead.

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:26.460
Others actually prefer the slower option.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:32.390
Say, for example, you have one day of meetings
or events to attend the next day — the sleeper

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:37.260
train saves you from the one night you’d
otherwise spend at a hotel anyway.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:43.100
Now, it would be wrong to say that demand
never materialized.

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:47.410
Just look at how busy stations get around
Chinese New Year.

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:52.220
But the undertaking can hardly be called a
success, either.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:57.980
Individual lines vary across the spectrum
from extremely popular to catastrophically

00:09:57.980 --> 00:09:59.410
empty.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:05.390
Although these numbers are wildly out of date,
pay attention to the difference between them.

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:10.780
While some routes saw twenty or thirty million
passengers a year, like Beijing to Shanghai,

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:16.120
or Shanghai to Nanjing, elsewhere, quote “The
scale of investment seems to be difficult

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:19.980
to justify”, says a Beijing professor of
economics.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:25.520
Zhengzhou to Xi'an is, on average, less than
50% full.

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:30.250
Huaibei to Shanghai, as low as 21%.

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:34.010
In fact, the majority of routes lose money.

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:39.750
According to the World Bank, out of 31 lines,
just five are at least break-even.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:45.950
Four were unable to pay back the principal
on their loans, eleven, also their interest,

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:50.650
and another eleven were unable even to cover
their operating costs.

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:57.150
The 1,700 km route connecting Xinjiang to
the capital of Gansu province, in the (largely

00:10:57.150 --> 00:11:04.770
empty) Gobi desert, runs just 8 of the 320
daily trains it was designed to handle.

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:10.070
It loses money even on electricity, meaning
there’s no hope of paying back its other

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:15.780
operating costs, to say nothing of the interest
on its loans, and you can forget about the

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:17.350
principal.

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:22.690
To keep the lights on, operators have continuously
taken on new debt to pay off the old in a

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:24.660
vicious cycle.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:28.710
No one is quite sure how this ends.

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:33.330
You might argue that high-speed rail doesn’t
need to be profitable — that it can exist

00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:36.690
purely as a “public good”.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:42.180
This may be true, but “efficiency” is
defined as output divided by input — how

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:45.950
much do you get out for every dollar or hour
you put in.

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:51.450
There’s no denying China’s incredible
output — the fruits of which are plainly

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:53.260
clear to see.

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:58.390
But too often are speed and scale marveled
at for their own sake — with no regard for

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:01.300
their cost or context.

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:04.890
The point is that China didn’t “crack
the code” on high-speed rail.

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:10.360
It didn’t discover or invent some new method
or technology that made it cheaper, faster,

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:11.360
or more widespread.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:18.370
Rather, it paid the price — a tremendous
price, and there’s no reason to think any

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:23.360
other country, including the U.S., couldn’t
accomplish the same thing if it similarly

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:26.350
abandoned all financial constraints.

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:33.200
$900 billion of debt can buy quite a lot of
rail.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:38.390
Even setting aside financial measures, it’s
unclear that high-speed rail had a net positive

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:39.890
effect.

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:45.250
Its singular, almost religious obsession with
building more and more and more high-speed

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:51.270
rail, well past its usefulness, has diverted
funds away from conventional rail, on which

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:53.620
freight trains depend.

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:58.610
While slow trains are certainly less exciting,
their underinvestment and, in some cases,

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:06.690
dismantling has led to a rise in road transport,
which is good for no one, not least, the planet.

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:11.480
This is not the image of a government free
from political considerations, quietly and

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:17.350
competently pursuing the most optimal policies
according to one grand strategic plan.

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:23.351
Instead, this, like many of its superlative-laced
megaprojects — The Three Gorges Dam, The

00:13:23.351 --> 00:13:30.730
Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macau bridge, and others
— are designed, first and foremost, to impress.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:35.990
They benefit from a fundamental psychological
tendency to view other, faraway countries

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:39.270
as possessing the solutions to our own.

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:45.120
If America can’t build cost-effective high-speed
rail because of endless red tape, its pervasiveness

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:50.520
in China must be evidence of success, rather
than a reflection of its own unique flavor

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:53.360
of bureaucratic inefficiency.

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:58.940
The mistake is assuming that because China
lacks your country’s exact problems that

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:01.500
it must be free of its own.

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:06.790
Now, all this inefficient development could’ve
been avoided if Xi Jinping had just taken

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:10.460
the right course on today’s sponsor, Skillshare.

